---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro'
import { RevealOnScroll } from '../components/RevealOnScroll'
import { lastUpdated, formatDate } from '../lib/lastUpdated.mjs'

const updatedDate = lastUpdated('src/pages/why.astro')

// When the facts about other projects (stars, versions, features) were last
// checked. Set by hand on purpose: the "Updated" date above moves on any edit,
// this one only when someone has actually re-checked the comparisons.
const FACTS_AS_OF = 'November 2026'
---
<BaseLayout title="Why Tome — How it compares to every self-hosted ebook server" description="An honest, opinionated comparison of Tome against Calibre-Web, Kavita, Komga, Grimmory (BookLore), BookOrbit and other self-hosted ebook servers.">
  <SiteHeader />

  <main>
  <article class="why-article">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <header class="relative max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-32 pb-16 text-center">
      <div class="accent-glow"></div>
      <p class="reveal inline-block mb-5 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[var(--accent)]">
        An opinionated comparison
      </p>
      <h1 class="reveal text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-6 leading-[1.1]">
        Why Tome?
      </h1>
      <p class="reveal text-lg md:text-xl text-[var(--muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        There are several good self-hosted ebook servers. This page explains what Tome does
        differently, how every alternative compares, and where each one is the better choice.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal mt-4 text-sm text-[var(--muted)] opacity-60">
        12 min read{updatedDate && <> · Updated <time datetime={updatedDate.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}>{formatDate(updatedDate)}</time></>}
      </p>
      <p class="reveal mt-6 text-sm text-[var(--muted)] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mx-auto">
        Star counts, versions and features of other projects are as of {FACTS_AS_OF}. They change;
        each project's own docs have the current state. If something here is wrong,
        please <a href="https://github.com/bndct-devops/tome/issues" class="underline hover:text-[var(--fg)]">open an issue</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- The problem -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Most self-hosted library servers were built to <em>serve files</em>. They're very good at
        it — scanning folders, extracting metadata, exposing an OPDS catalog, rendering pages
        in a browser. What they don't do is care whether you actually <em>read</em> any of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tome was built around a different premise: a library server should know how you read.
        When you pick up your Kobo at night and read for an hour, Tome should record that session,
        sync your position to the web reader, update your streak, and surface it in stats the next
        morning. That's the foundation — not a feature bolted on later.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Tome does -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="what-tome-does">What Tome does</h2>

      <h3>TomeSync — a real KOReader plugin, not just a protocol</h3>
      <p>
        Most self-hosted libraries that support KOReader do it the same way: they implement
        the KOSync protocol on the server side, and you point KOReader's built-in sync plugin at it.
        That gets you progress sync — where you left off — and nothing else. CWA ships a plugin, but
        it's a fork of the stock KOSync plugin with its own sign-in. Kavita, Komga,
        and Stump all just expose KOSync-compatible endpoints.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tome ships <strong>TomeSync</strong>, a purpose-built <code>.koplugin</code> that goes
        well beyond progress sync:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reading-session recording</strong> — every time you close the lid or close a book, TomeSync records a session: start time, end time, duration, pages turned, device. This is what powers Tome's stats (streaks, heatmaps, pace, completion estimates).</li>
        <li><strong>Series browser</strong> — browse your entire library's series from inside KOReader. Tap a series to bulk-download every volume, organized into folders by book type and series name (<code>manga/One Piece/Vol. 1 — One Piece.epub</code>). Or download just the rest of a series from your current volume forward.</li>
        <li><strong>Bidirectional position sync</strong> — read on your Kobo, pick up on the web reader, come back to KOReader the next night. Position follows you both directions automatically.</li>
        <li><strong>Stable book IDs</strong> — TomeSync matches books by Tome's internal IDs, not MD5 file hashes. Rename a file, re-download it, reorganize your library — the link doesn't break.</li>
        <li><strong>Offline-first</strong> — when your server is unreachable, sync silently skips with zero delay. Sessions are saved locally and flush automatically when the connection returns. Up to 50 sessions can queue offline.</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-configured</strong> — download the plugin from Tome's settings page and it comes with your server URL and API key baked in. No manual configuration.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Stats</h3>
      <p>
        Stats aren't an afterthought. Seven pages: Overview (reading time, streak, goals, what
        you're reading now, a 365-day grid, all-time records), Goals (each goal's pace, history and
        what it takes), Habits (hour-by-weekday grid, the
        last 14 days, sessions, pace, reading speed, reading character), Library (series, book
        types, authors, backlog, what you've added), Taste (your ratings), Timeline (every book as
        a bar across the years) and Sessions (every sitting, with trim and delete). All of it built
        on actual session data from your e-reader, not estimated from page counts.
      </p>

      <h3>Series</h3>
      <p>
        Books group into series, series have statuses (ongoing, completed, hiatus), and volumes
        can be organized into arcs within a series. This matters for manga and light novels where
        you might have 30+ volumes in a single series spread across multiple story arcs.
      </p>

      <h3>Free metadata</h3>
      <p>
        Metadata fetching is free and built in. Google Books, OpenLibrary, and Hardcover (with an
        optional API key) — no subscription required. The Scribe tool (a Claude Code skill) can
        batch-ingest hundreds of books, auto-match metadata, and audit your library for weak spots.
      </p>

      <h3>The stack</h3>
      <p>
        Python/FastAPI on the backend, React 19 with Tailwind CSS 4 on the frontend, SQLite under
        the hood. One Docker image, three volume mounts, no external services. It runs on a
        Raspberry Pi.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- What Tome doesn't do -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="what-tome-doesnt-do">What Tome doesn't do</h2>
      <p>
        Before getting into comparisons — an honest list of things other tools handle that Tome
        currently does not:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Calibre integration</strong> — Tome has its own database. It won't read an existing Calibre library.</li>
        <li><strong>Kobo device sync</strong> — in the works. Today Tome syncs via the TomeSync KOReader plugin, not the Kobo store API.</li>
        <li><strong>Format conversion</strong> — Tome serves what you upload.</li>
        <li><strong>LDAP</strong> — sign-in is local accounts or OIDC single sign-on, with three roles (admin, member, guest).</li>
        <li><strong>Reading lists</strong> — Tome has libraries and shelves (saved filters), not ordered cross-series playlists.</li>
        <li><strong>AniList / MAL scrobbling</strong> — Tome pushes ratings and progress to Hardcover, and nowhere else.</li>
        <li><strong>Age rating restrictions</strong> — visibility is library-based, not rating-based.</li>
        <li><strong>Audiobooks</strong> — ebooks, comics and manga only.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Some of these will land eventually. Some won't — they're outside the scope of what Tome is
        trying to be. If any of them are dealbreakers, keep reading — one of the tools below is a
        better fit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Calibre-Web -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="calibre-web">Calibre-Web</h2>
      <div class="why-meta">
        <span>18.6k stars</span>
        <span>Python / Flask / Bootstrap 3</span>
        <span>GPL-3.0</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/janeczku/calibre-web">Calibre-Web</a> is the default
        recommendation on every self-hosting forum, and for good reason. It wraps an existing
        Calibre database in a web UI, supports OPDS, has built-in readers for EPUB, PDF, CBZ,
        and even DJVU and audiobooks, offers send-to-Kindle, Google Drive hosting, LDAP and OAuth
        login, and speaks 20+ languages. Its ecosystem is massive.
      </p>
      <p>
        The trade-off is that Calibre-Web is fundamentally a <em>database viewer</em>. It reads
        Calibre's <code>metadata.db</code> and presents it in a browser. The UI is Bootstrap 3
        (released 2013) with Jinja2 server-rendered templates. There are no reading sessions, no
        time tracking, no streaks, no stats beyond "how many books and authors do I have." The
        web EPUB reader keeps your automatic position in browser localStorage, not on the server
        (signed-in users can save one manual bookmark per book). Switch browsers and the automatic
        position is gone.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's no native KOReader support. KOSync doesn't exist in stock Calibre-Web. It does
        have Kobo device sync (emulating the Kobo store API), which is a feature Tome doesn't have.
      </p>

      <h3 id="calibre-web-automated">The CWA fork</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/crocodilestick/Calibre-Web-Automated">Calibre-Web Automated</a> (6.7k stars) is a more ambitious fork that adds a lot of what stock Calibre-Web lacks:
        KOSync support with a downloadable KOReader plugin, automatic file ingest with format
        conversion, metadata enforcement that writes changes back into actual ebook files, smart
        duplicate detection, magic shelves with dynamic rules, and a multi-tab stats center with
        ECharts charts and CSV export. It's a serious project.
      </p>
      <p>
        But it's still built on the same Flask/Bootstrap 3 foundation. The roadmap lists a
        Svelte-based frontend rewrite as a high priority, which tells you where the current UI
        stands. It also still runs on a Calibre <code>metadata.db</code>, though it now creates an
        empty one for you when none is found. Tome has its own database schema and doesn't depend on any external binary.
      </p>

      <div class="why-verdict">
        <strong>Pick Calibre-Web if</strong> you already live in the Calibre ecosystem, want
        send-to-Kindle, need LDAP/OAuth, or want the broadest format support (DJVU, audiobooks).
        Pick the CWA fork if you want that plus auto-import, format conversion, and KOSync.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Kavita -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="kavita">Kavita</h2>
      <div class="why-meta">
        <span>12.2k stars</span>
        <span>C# / .NET / Angular / SQLite</span>
        <span>GPL-3.0</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/Kareadita/Kavita">Kavita</a> is the most feature-rich
        self-hosted reading server available. It has everything: built-in readers for EPUB,
        comics, and PDF, OPDS with page streaming, reading lists, collections, smart filters
        with 30+ criteria, dashboard streams, EPUB annotations and highlights, reading profiles,
        age rating restrictions, OIDC single sign-on, custom themes with a community theme
        browser, real-time UI updates via SignalR, and i18n.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since v0.8.9 (January 2026), Kavita also tracks reading sessions — time spent, pages read,
        words read, daily activity graphs, and reading pace. It's one of the few other
        self-hosted tools that take session tracking seriously.
      </p>
      <p>
        KOReader sync landed in v0.8.7 (July 2025) for EPUBs, with comics and PDF following in
        v0.8.9. It uses the standard KOSync protocol: server-side endpoints that KOReader's
        built-in sync connects to, with no first-party plugin. Worth noting: Kavita's own wiki includes the warning <em>"The automatic sync can potentially undo reading progress. We strongly advise against
        using it."</em> Books are matched by a hash of the file's content, so a
        re-downloaded or modified file loses the link. Progress pushed from KOReader does feed
        Kavita's reading sessions, but they are derived on the server from progress updates, not
        recorded on the device. Browsing from the device goes through OPDS. Kobo sync is in the
        nightly builds for v0.9.2.
      </p>
      <p>
        The biggest caveat is metadata. Kavita's free tier only reads embedded metadata from your
        files (ComicInfo.xml, OPF). External metadata fetching — looking up covers, descriptions,
        and tags from MangaBaka, Hardcover and ComicBookRoundup (the sources since v0.9.1) — requires
        <strong>Kavita+</strong>, a paid subscription. Scrobbling to AniList, MAL, Hardcover and
        MangaBaka is part of Kavita+ too. Tome fetches metadata from
        Hardcover, Google Books and OpenLibrary for free, no subscription needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kavita is a .NET/Angular application. If you're comfortable with that stack and want the
        widest feature set, it's an excellent choice. It does things Tome doesn't: reading
        lists, age rating restrictions, AniList scrobbling and a community theme browser.
      </p>

      <div class="why-verdict">
        <strong>Pick Kavita if</strong> you want the most features in a single package, need
        reading lists or AniList scrobbling, or manage a library with age-restricted
        content. Be aware that external metadata and scrobbling require a paid subscription.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Komga -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="komga">Komga</h2>
      <div class="why-meta">
        <span>6.9k stars</span>
        <span>Kotlin / Spring Boot / Vue / SQLite</span>
        <span>MIT</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/gotson/komga">Komga</a> is purpose-built for comics and
        manga, and within that niche it's arguably the best tool available. Its comic reader
        supports left-to-right, right-to-left, vertical scroll, and webtoon modes with spread
        layouts. It has Collections (group related series) and Read Lists (ordered book playlists
        across series). It handles one-shots as a first-class concept, detects duplicate pages
        within archives, and offers OPDS v1.2 with page streaming plus OPDS v2.
      </p>
      <p>
        KOReader sync is supported via the standard KOSync protocol (no custom plugin) with a
        significant limitation: EPUB progress syncs at <em>chapter boundaries only</em>, not
        mid-chapter. Comics and other pre-paginated books sync per page. No session recording,
        no series downloading from the device. Kobo sync is also built in (EPUB only).
      </p>
      <p>
        What Komga doesn't have: stats. No reading sessions, no time tracking, no heatmaps, no
        reading pace, no streak counter. The only insight it offers is read/unread/in-progress
        badges. It also has no external metadata fetching — no Google Books, no OpenLibrary, no
        AniList lookups. Metadata comes entirely from embedded and local sources (ComicInfo.xml, EPUB
        OPF, Mylar series.json, ISBN barcodes).
      </p>
      <p>
        The classic frontend is Vue 2 with Vuetify 2 (Vue 2 reached end-of-life in December 2023).
        A new Vue 3 interface has been in beta since v1.26 (August 2026) and can be made the
        default, though it has no reader of its own yet. The EPUB reader uses a Readium 2 fork and
        is functional but minimal compared to dedicated EPUB tools (three themes: day, sepia and
        night). Development is steady with a single dedicated maintainer and only 5 open issues,
        which speaks to code quality.
      </p>

      <div class="why-verdict">
        <strong>Pick Komga if</strong> your library is primarily comics and manga, you want the
        best comic reader experience, or you value a clean MIT-licensed codebase with very few
        open issues. Don't pick it for ebooks or if you care about reading stats.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Grimmory (BookLore) -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="grimmory">Grimmory (BookLore)</h2>
      <div class="why-meta">
        <span>4.8k stars</span>
        <span>Java / TypeScript / MariaDB</span>
        <span>AGPL-3.0</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/grimmory-tools/grimmory">Grimmory</a> is the community-run
        continuation of the BookLore codebase. The original BookLore repository went offline
        without notice in March 2026, and former contributors forked the code as Grimmory a few
        days later. They have kept it moving since, with regular releases (v3.5.1 in November
        2026). The BookLore repository has since returned, but has seen no feature work since
        September 2026. If you ran BookLore and want the same application with ongoing releases,
        Grimmory is the closest thing to what you had.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a broad all-in-one library. Its README lists rule-based smart shelves with full-text
        search, metadata from Google Books, Open Library and Amazon, a browser reader for EPUB,
        PDF and comics with annotations and highlights, Kobo device sync, OPDS, KOReader progress
        sync, OIDC sign-in, a BookDrop folder for imports, and send-to-Kindle or email. It reads
        formats Tome does not (AZW3, FB2, CB7) and handles audiobooks (M4B, MP3, OPUS). It
        has reading statistics too: 19 charts built from reading sessions, among them a session
        heatmap with streaks, a timeline and peak reading hours.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first difference is the shape of the install. Grimmory is a Java application with a
        MariaDB server next to it: two containers and a set of database credentials in the compose
        file. Tome is one container with SQLite inside, and a backup is a copy of one folder.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is the e-reader. Grimmory syncs reading progress with KOReader, and has its own
        KOReader plugin that reads books from the server, syncs shelves and tracks progress; its
        README labels it alpha. Tome's plugin records every sitting on the device (start, end,
        pages turned) and keeps them when the server is out of reach, lets you browse and download
        whole series from the reader, and brings your highlights back to the library. Tome's stats
        are built from those device sessions.
      </p>
      <div class="why-verdict">
        <strong>Pick Grimmory if</strong> you own a Kobo and want native Kobo sync, keep audiobooks
        or AZW3 files in the same library, or are coming from BookLore and want
        everything to stay where it was. Pick Tome if you read in KOReader and want one small
        container.
      </div>

      <h3 id="coming-from-booklore">Coming from BookLore</h3>
      <p>
        Tome does not import a BookLore or Grimmory database. What carries over is the files:
        mount your existing book folder and run a scan, and Tome reads the metadata embedded in
        each file and can fetch the rest. Reading history, shelves and user accounts do not carry
        over. The <a href="/docs/installation">installation guide</a> and
        the <a href="/docs/first-run">first-run setup</a> page cover it in a few minutes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- BookOrbit -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="bookorbit">BookOrbit</h2>
      <div class="why-meta">
        <span>6.8k stars</span>
        <span>TypeScript / Vue / PostgreSQL</span>
        <span>AGPL-3.0</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/bookorbit/bookorbit">BookOrbit</a> is a self-hosted library for
        ebooks, PDFs, comics and audiobooks. It is made by BookLore's original developer and
        presented as BookLore's successor. The repository has been public since May 2026.
      </p>
      <p>
        It covers ebooks and audiobooks, Kobo sync, a KOReader plugin, an iPhone and Apple Watch
        app, reading statistics, and sync to Hardcover, Readwise and StoryGraph.
      </p>
      <p>
        It runs as an app container plus a PostgreSQL server. Its README states that NAS and
        network storage are not supported for any writable path, the book library included. Some
        features are exclusive to its official iOS and Android apps: the server routes behind them
        answer only to a verified copy of the official app. Its open API and third-party clients
        are otherwise unaffected.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tome is one container with SQLite inside and reads your library from wherever it is
        mounted, a network share included; only its own data folder should sit on local disk. Its
        iPhone app goes through the same public API any other client can use. It has no
        audiobooks, no Kobo sync and no download automation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Other tools -->
    <section class="why-section reveal">
      <h2 id="others">Other tools worth knowing about</h2>
      <p>
        The five above are the main alternatives for a general-purpose self-hosted ebook library.
        These are more specialised:
      </p>

      <h3 id="audiobookshelf">Audiobookshelf</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/advplyr/audiobookshelf">Audiobookshelf</a> (15.1k stars) is
        the dominant self-hosted audiobook server, and it handles ebooks too — EPUB, PDF, and
        CBZ/CBR. But its own README calls it "Basic ebook support and ereader." There's no OPDS, no
        KOReader sync, and the stats system is built around listening time, not reading. If your
        library is primarily audiobooks with some ebooks on the side, it's a great choice. For an
        ebook-first library, look elsewhere.
      </p>

      <h3 id="stump">Stump</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/stumpapp/stump">Stump</a> (2.8k stars) is the most
        interesting newcomer. It's written in Rust with a React frontend, has KOReader and Kobo
        sync, OPDS v1.2 and experimental v2, reading session tracking, and a clean architecture.
        The README is honest: Stump <em>"should be treated as beta software until it reaches a
        stable 1.0 release."</em> At v0.1.14 (November 2026) it is still early, but it's one to
        watch.
      </p>

      <h3 id="lanraragi">LANraragi</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/Difegue/LANraragi">LANraragi</a> (3.2k stars) has been around
        for 12 years and is excellent at one thing: managing manga and doujinshi archives. It has
        a plugin system for metadata scraping, tag namespaces, Prometheus metrics export, and OPDS
        with page streaming. The limitation is single-user only — one admin password for the whole
        instance. If you're a solo manga reader with a large archive, it's battle-tested.
      </p>

      <h3 id="ubooquity">Ubooquity</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="https://vaemendis.net/ubooquity/">Ubooquity</a> is a closed-source Java
        application that was popular years ago. It is still alive: 3.1.0, the first stable v3,
        came out in August 2025, the LinuxServer.io Docker image is maintained and the docs now
        live on GitHub Pages. Releases are rare, though, and there's no public source code. Hard
        to recommend for a new setup over the open-source options.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Closing -->
    <section class="why-section reveal" style="border-bottom: none;">
      <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
      <p>
        Tome is free, open-source (AGPL-3.0), and deploys in under a minute.
      </p>
      <div class="why-cta">
        <a href="/docs/installation" class="why-cta-btn primary">Installation guide</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/bndct-devops/tome" class="why-cta-btn">View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
  </main>

  <RevealOnScroll client:load />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .why-article {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 24px 80px;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }
  .why-section {
    padding-bottom: 48px;
    margin-bottom: 48px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .why-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .why-section h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 32px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .why-section p {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .why-section a {
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent-soft);
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    transition: text-decoration-color 0.15s;
  }
  .why-section a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--accent);
  }
  .why-section ul {
    padding-left: 1.5em;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .why-section li {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .why-section li::marker {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .why-section code {
    font-size: 0.88em;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .why-section em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .why-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .why-meta span {
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .why-verdict {
    margin-top: 20px;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    background: var(--card);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
